//! TLS 1.3 client: handshake driver and record-layer front end.
//!
//! This file is the client half of Nova's pure-Nova TLS 1.3 stack (RFC 8446).
//! It owns the per-connection state machine that a caller drives message by
//! message: build a ClientHello, feed the server's flight in, verify the
//! certificate and Finished, derive the traffic keys, and then encrypt and
//! decrypt application records. All the cryptography (X25519/P-256/P-384 ECDHE,
//! HKDF key schedule, AEAD record protection, X.509 chain verification, ECDSA)
//! lives in the sibling modules imported below; this file is the wire-format
//! and sequencing layer that stitches them into a working handshake.
//!
//! Design shape. The whole module works over raw byte buffers (`ptr`) rather
//! than owned Nova strings or lists, because it sits directly on the socket
//! read and write path where every avoidable copy or allocation costs
//! throughput. Buffers are passed in by the caller, written in place, and the
//! returned `int` is almost always the number of bytes produced or consumed.
//! Multi-byte integers are big-endian (network byte order) throughout, encoded
//! by hand with shifts and masks. The recurring `putHex` helper lets fixed
//! extension blobs be written as their exact on-the-wire hex, which keeps the
//! ClientHello construction readable against the RFC.
//!
//! Pointer discipline. Buffer addresses are always held as `ptr` (or `long`
//! when arithmetic is needed): an `int` would truncate any heap address above
//! 4 GB and silently corrupt the pointer. Offsets into a buffer are plain `int`
//! because `bytes.read_byte`/`bytes.write_byte` compute the final address at 64
//! bits internally, so `base + offset` never overflows even when `offset` is an
//! `int`. The `at` helper materialises an interior pointer safely by doing the
//! addition in `long`.
//!
//! The transcript hash. TLS 1.3 binds every handshake secret to the running
//! hash of all handshake messages seen so far ("the transcript"). [`TlsClient`]
//! accumulates the raw messages into `trBuf` and hashes them on demand with the
//! negotiated hash (SHA-256 for most suites, SHA-384 for TLS_AES_256_GCM,
//! tracked by `kind`). Every step that consumes or emits a handshake message
//! also calls [`trAdd`] so the transcript stays in lockstep with the peer; a
//! HelloRetryRequest additionally rewrites the transcript to the special
//! synthetic "message_hash" form that RFC 8446 section 4.4.1 mandates.
//!
//! What the state machine covers. Full 1-RExtT handshakes, HelloRetryRequest
//! with cookie and key-share group change, session resumption via PSK
//! (NewSessionTicket + the resumption binder), 0-RTT early data, optional
//! client certificate authentication (CertificateRequest, Certificate,
//! CertificateVerify), OCSP stapling, post-handshake KeyUpdate, and the
//! downgrade-sentinel and record-size-limit checks. Two entry points feed the
//! server flight: [`processFlight`] parses a single already-decrypted record,
//! while [`feedHandshake`] is the streaming path that unprotects records itself
//! and reassembles handshake messages that span record boundaries.
//!
//! This module is consumed by the higher-level TLS connection wrapper in the
//! runtime's async socket layer, which does the actual socket I/O and calls
//! these functions in handshake order. It does no I/O and no allocation beyond
//! scratch buffers it frees before returning, so it is deterministic and easy
//! to test against RFC vectors.

import bytes;
import crypto.tls.13.tls;
import crypto.tls.13.handshake;
import crypto.ecc.x25519;
import crypto.ecc.p256;
import crypto.ecc.p384;
import crypto.x509;
import crypto.hash.sha256;
import crypto.hash.sha512;
import crypto.mac.hmac;

/// Returns an interior pointer `off` bytes into `buf`.
///
/// The addition is done in `long` so a byte offset added to a heap address
/// above 4 GB does not truncate. Used wherever a function needs to hand a
/// sub-buffer (a message inside a flight, a leaf inside a certificate list) to
/// another routine that expects a base pointer.
fn at(buf: ptr, off: int): ptr { return (buf as long + off) as ptr; }

/// Decodes a single lowercase-or-digit hex character to its 0..15 nibble value.
///
/// Digits '0'..'9' map by subtracting 48; letters 'a'..'f' map by subtracting
/// 87 (that is, 'a' is 97, so 97 - 87 = 10). Only lowercase hex is expected,
/// since every caller passes a literal lowercase string. There is no validation
/// for out-of-range input.
fn chNib(c: int): int {
    if (c >= 48 && c <= 57) { return c - 48; }
    return c - 87;
}
/// Writes the bytes decoded from a hex string into `out` at offset `p`, and
/// returns the new offset just past them.
///
/// This is the workhorse for emitting fixed, pre-computed extension blobs (the
/// supported_versions, supported_groups, signature_algorithms and similar
/// records) as their exact on-the-wire hex, which keeps the ClientHello builder
/// legible against RFC 8446. Each pair of hex characters becomes one byte, so
/// the string length must be even; `chNib` does the per-character decode.
fn putHex(out: ptr, p: int, hex: string): int {
    let n = hex.length / 2;
    let i = 0;
    while (i < n) { bytes.write_byte(out, p + i, chNib(hex[i * 2]) * 16 + chNib(hex[i * 2 + 1])); i = i + 1; }
    return p + n;
}

/// Writes the ALPN (application_layer_protocol_negotiation, extension 0x0010)
/// extension into `out` at offset `p`, returning the offset past it.
///
/// `alpn` is a comma-separated list of protocol names (for example
/// "h2,http/1.1"); each name is emitted as a one-byte length prefix followed by
/// its bytes, inside the ALPN protocol-name list, inside the extension body.
/// The two length fields (extension length and list length) are back-patched
/// once the names have been written, since their totals are not known up front.
/// An empty `alpn` writes nothing and returns `p` unchanged, so the extension
/// is simply omitted.
fn putAlpn(out: ptr, p: int, alpn: string): int {
    if (alpn.length == 0) { return p; }
    bytes.write_byte(out, p, 0x00); bytes.write_byte(out, p + 1, 0x10); p = p + 2;
    let extLenPos = p; p = p + 2;
    let listLenPos = p; p = p + 2;
    let listStart = p;
    let i = 0;
    while (i < alpn.length) {
        let j = i;
        while (j < alpn.length && alpn[j] != 44) { j = j + 1; }
        let tlen = j - i;
        if (tlen > 0) {
            bytes.write_byte(out, p, tlen); p = p + 1;
            let k = 0;
            while (k < tlen) { bytes.write_byte(out, p + k, alpn[i + k]); k = k + 1; }
            p = p + tlen;
        }
        i = j + 1;
    }
    let listLen = p - listStart;
    bytes.write_byte(out, listLenPos, (listLen >> 8) & 0xff); bytes.write_byte(out, listLenPos + 1, listLen & 0xff);
    let extLen = p - (extLenPos + 2);
    bytes.write_byte(out, extLenPos, (extLen >> 8) & 0xff); bytes.write_byte(out, extLenPos + 1, extLen & 0xff);
    return p;
}

/// Writes the SNI (server_name, extension 0x0000) extension naming `host` into
/// `out` at offset `p`, returning the offset past it.
///
/// The extension carries a server_name_list of exactly one host_name entry
/// (name type 0), so all three length fields are derived directly from the host
/// length: extension body is `5 + hostlen`, the list is `3 + hostlen`, and the
/// name itself is `hostlen`. The leading "0000" written via `putHex` is the
/// extension type. Unlike ALPN nothing needs back-patching, because there is
/// only one entry of known length.
fn putSni(out: ptr, p: int, host: string): int {
    let hl = host.length;
    p = putHex(out, p, "0000");
    bytes.write_byte(out, p, ((5 + hl) >> 8) & 0xff); bytes.write_byte(out, p + 1, (5 + hl) & 0xff); p = p + 2;
    bytes.write_byte(out, p, ((3 + hl) >> 8) & 0xff); bytes.write_byte(out, p + 1, (3 + hl) & 0xff); p = p + 2;
    bytes.write_byte(out, p, 0); p = p + 1;
    bytes.write_byte(out, p, (hl >> 8) & 0xff); bytes.write_byte(out, p + 1, hl & 0xff); p = p + 2;
    let i = 0;
    while (i < hl) { bytes.write_byte(out, p + i, host[i]); i = i + 1; }
    return p + hl;
}

/// Builds a plain ClientHello (no HelloRetryRequest cookie, no PSK, no early
/// data) into `out` and returns its length in bytes.
///
/// This is the common entry point for a fresh full handshake. It offers only
/// the X25519 and P-256 key shares (`groupMask` 3) and forwards to
/// [`buildClientHelloEx`] with all the resumption and early-data parameters
/// zeroed. `random` and `sessionId` must each point at 32 bytes; `ephPub` and
/// `ephPubP256` are the client's ephemeral public keys for the two groups. The
/// one-byte `empty` scratch buffer stands in for the unused P-384 share and PSK
/// arguments and is freed before returning.
pub fn buildClientHello(random: ptr, sessionId: ptr, ephPub: ptr, ephPubP256: ptr, host: string, alpn: string, out: ptr): int {
    let empty = bytes.alloc(1);
    let n = buildClientHelloEx(random, sessionId, ephPub, ephPubP256, empty, host, alpn, 3, empty, 0, empty, 0, 0, 0, 0, out);
    bytes.free(empty);
    return n;
}

/// Builds a fully parameterised ClientHello into `out` and returns its length.
///
/// This is the complete builder that every code path funnels through. The
/// message is laid out starting at offset 4 to leave room for the 1-byte
/// handshake type plus 3-byte length header, which are back-patched at the very
/// end once `bodyLen` is known. In order it writes: the legacy version 0x0303,
/// the 32-byte client random, the 32-byte legacy session id, the cipher-suite
/// list (TLS_AES_128_GCM / TLS_AES_256_GCM / TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305), the null
/// compression method, then the extension block.
///
/// The extensions cover SNI, supported_versions (0x2b, TLS 1.3 only),
/// supported_groups, signature_algorithms, psk_key_exchange_modes,
/// record_size_limit, and (only when not resuming with a binder) padding, ALPN,
/// an optional HelloRetryRequest cookie, the key_share block gated by
/// `groupMask` (bit 0 = X25519, bit 1 = P-256, bit 2 = P-384), an optional
/// early_data indicator, and finally the pre_shared_key extension.
///
/// The pre_shared_key extension MUST come last per RFC 8446, and its binder is
/// written as `binderLen` zero bytes here as a placeholder: the real binder can
/// only be computed once the rest of the message exists, so [`fillResumptionBinder`]
/// patches it in afterwards. `obfAge` is the obfuscated ticket age (ticket age
/// plus the server's `ticket_age_add`). All the length fields for the key_share
/// list, the PSK identities, and the outer extension block are back-patched
/// after their contents are emitted.
pub fn buildClientHelloEx(random: ptr, sessionId: ptr, ephPub: ptr, ephPubP256: ptr, ephPubP384: ptr, host: string, alpn: string,
                          groupMask: int, cookie: ptr, cookieLen: int,
                          pskTicket: ptr, pskTicketLen: int, obfAge: long, binderLen: int, earlyData: int, out: ptr): int {
    let p = 4;
    bytes.write_byte(out, p, 0x03); bytes.write_byte(out, p + 1, 0x03); p = p + 2;
    let i = 0;
    while (i < 32) { bytes.write_byte(out, p + i, bytes.read_byte(random, i)); i = i + 1; }
    p = p + 32;
    bytes.write_byte(out, p, 0x20); p = p + 1;
    i = 0;
    while (i < 32) { bytes.write_byte(out, p + i, bytes.read_byte(sessionId, i)); i = i + 1; }
    p = p + 32;
    p = putHex(out, p, "0006130113021303");
    p = putHex(out, p, "0100");

    let extLenPos = p; p = p + 2;
    let extStart = p;
    p = putSni(out, p, host);
    p = putHex(out, p, "002b0003020304");
    p = putHex(out, p, "000a00080006001d00170018");
    p = putHex(out, p, "000d00080006080404030401");
    p = putHex(out, p, "002d00020101");
    p = putHex(out, p, "001c00024001");
    if (binderLen == 0) { p = putHex(out, p, "000500050100000000"); }
    p = putAlpn(out, p, alpn);
    if (cookieLen > 0) {
        bytes.write_byte(out, p, 0x00); bytes.write_byte(out, p + 1, 0x2c); p = p + 2;
        let cExt = cookieLen + 2;
        bytes.write_byte(out, p, (cExt >> 8) & 0xff); bytes.write_byte(out, p + 1, cExt & 0xff); p = p + 2;
        bytes.write_byte(out, p, (cookieLen >> 8) & 0xff); bytes.write_byte(out, p + 1, cookieLen & 0xff); p = p + 2;
        let ci = 0;
        while (ci < cookieLen) { bytes.write_byte(out, p + ci, bytes.read_byte(cookie, ci)); ci = ci + 1; }
        p = p + cookieLen;
    }
    bytes.write_byte(out, p, 0x00); bytes.write_byte(out, p + 1, 0x33); p = p + 2;
    let ksExtLenPos = p; p = p + 2;
    let ksListLenPos = p; p = p + 2;
    let ksListStart = p;
    if ((groupMask & 1) != 0) {
        p = putHex(out, p, "001d0020");
        i = 0;
        while (i < 32) { bytes.write_byte(out, p + i, bytes.read_byte(ephPub, i)); i = i + 1; }
        p = p + 32;
    }
    if ((groupMask & 2) != 0) {
        p = putHex(out, p, "00170041");
        i = 0;
        while (i < 65) { bytes.write_byte(out, p + i, bytes.read_byte(ephPubP256, i)); i = i + 1; }
        p = p + 65;
    }
    if ((groupMask & 4) != 0) {
        p = putHex(out, p, "00180061");
        i = 0;
        while (i < 97) { bytes.write_byte(out, p + i, bytes.read_byte(ephPubP384, i)); i = i + 1; }
        p = p + 97;
    }
    let ksListLen = p - ksListStart;
    bytes.write_byte(out, ksListLenPos, (ksListLen >> 8) & 0xff); bytes.write_byte(out, ksListLenPos + 1, ksListLen & 0xff);
    let ksExtLen = p - (ksExtLenPos + 2);
    bytes.write_byte(out, ksExtLenPos, (ksExtLen >> 8) & 0xff); bytes.write_byte(out, ksExtLenPos + 1, ksExtLen & 0xff);

    if (earlyData != 0) { p = putHex(out, p, "002a0000"); }
    if (binderLen > 0) {
        bytes.write_byte(out, p, 0x00); bytes.write_byte(out, p + 1, 0x29); p = p + 2;
        let idLen = 2 + pskTicketLen + 4;
        let bindersLen = 1 + binderLen;
        let pskExtLen = 2 + idLen + 2 + bindersLen;
        bytes.write_byte(out, p, (pskExtLen >> 8) & 0xff); bytes.write_byte(out, p + 1, pskExtLen & 0xff); p = p + 2;
        bytes.write_byte(out, p, (idLen >> 8) & 0xff); bytes.write_byte(out, p + 1, idLen & 0xff); p = p + 2;
        bytes.write_byte(out, p, (pskTicketLen >> 8) & 0xff); bytes.write_byte(out, p + 1, pskTicketLen & 0xff); p = p + 2;
        let ti = 0;
        while (ti < pskTicketLen) { bytes.write_byte(out, p + ti, bytes.read_byte(pskTicket, ti)); ti = ti + 1; }
        p = p + pskTicketLen;
        bytes.write_byte(out, p, ((obfAge >> 24) & 0xff) as int); bytes.write_byte(out, p + 1, ((obfAge >> 16) & 0xff) as int);
        bytes.write_byte(out, p + 2, ((obfAge >> 8) & 0xff) as int); bytes.write_byte(out, p + 3, (obfAge & 0xff) as int); p = p + 4;
        bytes.write_byte(out, p, (bindersLen >> 8) & 0xff); bytes.write_byte(out, p + 1, bindersLen & 0xff); p = p + 2;
        bytes.write_byte(out, p, binderLen); p = p + 1;
        let bz = 0;
        while (bz < binderLen) { bytes.write_byte(out, p + bz, 0); bz = bz + 1; }
        p = p + binderLen;
    }

    let extLen = p - extStart;
    bytes.write_byte(out, extLenPos, (extLen >> 8) & 0xff);
    bytes.write_byte(out, extLenPos + 1, extLen & 0xff);

    let bodyLen = p - 4;
    bytes.write_byte(out, 0, 0x01);
    bytes.write_byte(out, 1, (bodyLen >> 16) & 0xff);
    bytes.write_byte(out, 2, (bodyLen >> 8) & 0xff);
    bytes.write_byte(out, 3, bodyLen & 0xff);
    return p;
}

/// Reports whether the server's ServerHello random carries the TLS 1.3
/// downgrade sentinel that RFC 8446 section 4.1.3 defines.
///
/// A genuine TLS 1.3 server, when it has to fall back, stamps a fixed 8-byte
/// value ("DOWNGRD" plus a trailing 0x00 or 0x01) into the last 8 bytes of the
/// ServerHello random. A TLS 1.3 client that sees it MUST abort, because it
/// signals a man-in-the-middle forcing a downgrade. The check reads the 7-byte
/// "444f574e475244" tag at offset 30 in the ServerHello message (bytes 30..36,
/// the start of the last 8 random bytes) and accepts a final byte of 0 or 1.
pub fn hasDowngradeSentinel(shMsg: ptr): bool {
    let tag = "444f574e47524400";
    let i = 0;
    while (i < 7) {
        if (bytes.read_byte(shMsg, 30 + i) != chNib(tag[i * 2]) * 16 + chNib(tag[i * 2 + 1])) { return false; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    let last = bytes.read_byte(shMsg, 37);
    return last == 0 || last == 1;
}

/// Reports whether a ServerHello is really a HelloRetryRequest in disguise.
///
/// TLS 1.3 encodes HelloRetryRequest as an ordinary ServerHello whose random is
/// a fixed magic value: the SHA-256 of the string "HelloRetryRequest". This
/// checks the 32 bytes at offset 6 (the random starts after the 4-byte
/// handshake header and the 2-byte legacy version) against that known constant.
/// A caller that gets `true` must run [`processHelloRetryRequest`] and resend an
/// adjusted ClientHello rather than treat the message as a normal server hello.
pub fn isHelloRetryRequest(shMsg: ptr): bool {
    let hrr = "cf21ad74e59a6111be1d8c021e65b891c2a211167abb8c5e079e09e2c8a8339c";
    let i = 0;
    while (i < 32) {
        let want = chNib(hrr[i * 2]) * 16 + chNib(hrr[i * 2 + 1]);
        if (bytes.read_byte(shMsg, 6 + i) != want) { return false; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

/// All the mutable state for one TLS 1.3 client connection.
///
/// A caller creates one with [`newClient`] (or [`newClientResume`] for a PSK
/// handshake), then drives it through the handshake and into the data phase.
/// The struct owns a family of heap scratch buffers (the `ptr` fields), which
/// are allocated once in the constructor and reused across the connection; the
/// handshake helpers that need short-lived working space allocate and free it
/// locally rather than touching these. Because the buffers are owned raw
/// pointers, this struct must not be copied casually: the connection code holds
/// a single instance for the connection's lifetime.
///
/// Field naming follows TLS terminology: `c*` fields are client-write, `s*`
/// fields are server-write, `hs` is handshake-phase, `ap` is application-phase,
/// `cap`/`sap` are the client/server application traffic secrets.
struct TlsClient {
    /// Hash-suite selector for the key schedule: 1 means SHA-384 (used by
    /// TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384), anything else means SHA-256. Drives
    /// [`trHash`], [`trHashLen`] and every HKDF-Expand-Label call.
    pub kind: int,
    /// AEAD cipher-suite selector: 1 = TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384, 2 =
    /// TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256, 0 = TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256 (the default).
    /// Passed to the record-protection routines to pick key length and AEAD.
    pub suite: int,
    /// Client's ephemeral X25519 private key (the scalar), used for the ECDHE
    /// against the server's X25519 share.
    pub eph: ptr,
    /// Client's ephemeral P-256 private key, used when the server selects the
    /// secp256r1 group.
    pub ephP256: ptr,
    /// Client's ephemeral P-384 private key, used when the server selects the
    /// secp384r1 group.
    pub ephP384: ptr,
    /// Growable transcript buffer: the concatenation of every handshake message
    /// exchanged, in order. `trLen` is the used length and `trCap` the current
    /// capacity; [`trAdd`] appends and grows it, [`trHash`] hashes the first
    /// `trLen` bytes.
    pub trBuf: ptr, pub trLen: int, pub trCap: int,
    /// The handshake secret (HKDF stage after Extract with the ECDHE input).
    /// Both handshake traffic secrets and, later, the master secret derive from
    /// this.
    pub hs: ptr,
    /// Client and server handshake-traffic secrets ("c hs traffic" / "s hs
    /// traffic"), from which the handshake record keys and IVs come.
    pub chs: ptr, pub shs: ptr,
    /// Client handshake-phase record key and IV, used to protect the client's
    /// own handshake records (for example the client Certificate flight).
    pub cKey: ptr, pub cIv: ptr,
    /// Server handshake-phase record key and IV, used to unprotect the server's
    /// EncryptedExtensions/Certificate/Finished flight.
    pub sKey: ptr, pub sIv: ptr,
    /// The master secret (final HKDF stage), input to the application traffic
    /// secrets and the resumption master secret.
    pub master: ptr,
    /// Client and server application-traffic secrets ("c ap traffic" / "s ap
    /// traffic"). Also rotated in place on a KeyUpdate.
    pub cap: ptr, pub sap: ptr,
    /// Client application-phase record key and IV, used by [`encryptApp`] and
    /// friends.
    pub capKey: ptr, pub capIv: ptr,
    /// Server application-phase record key and IV, used by [`decryptApp`].
    pub sapKey: ptr, pub sapIv: ptr,
    /// AEAD record sequence numbers for the server-write and client-write
    /// directions. Each is the nonce counter for its direction and resets to 0
    /// on a key change (handshake-to-application transition, KeyUpdate).
    pub sSeq: long, pub cSeq: long,
    /// Set true once the server's CertificateVerify signature checks out
    /// against the transcript and the leaf certificate's key.
    pub certVerifyOk: bool,
    /// Set true once the server's Finished MAC matches the expected verify
    /// data. Together with `certVerifyOk` this gates handshake success.
    pub finishedOk: bool,
    /// Streaming handshake-reassembly buffer used by [`feedHandshake`]:
    /// decrypted handshake bytes accumulate here across record boundaries.
    /// `hsCap` is capacity, `hsLen` the bytes written, `hsPos` the parse
    /// cursor for messages already consumed.
    pub hsBuf: ptr, pub hsCap: int, pub hsLen: int, pub hsPos: int,
    /// Offset and length, within `hsBuf`, of the server's leaf (end-entity)
    /// certificate DER. Set while parsing the Certificate message so the leaf
    /// can be handed to X.509 verification.
    pub leafOff: int, pub leafLen: int,
    /// Offset and length, within `hsBuf`, of the full certificate_list body
    /// (all certificates plus their entry extensions), passed to
    /// [`verifyServerChain`].
    pub certListOff: int, pub certListLen: int,
    /// The ALPN protocol the server selected, or "" if none. Exposed via
    /// [`selectedAlpn`].
    pub alpnSel: string,
    /// Cookie echoed from a HelloRetryRequest and its length; must be replayed
    /// in the retried ClientHello. Owned buffer, reallocated when a cookie
    /// arrives.
    pub hrrCookie: ptr, pub hrrCookieLen: int,
    /// The resumption master secret, derived after the client Finished; the
    /// base for the PSK carried by a future NewSessionTicket.
    pub resMaster: ptr,
    /// The session ticket bytes from a NewSessionTicket and their length, to be
    /// presented as a PSK identity on a later resumption.
    pub ticket: ptr, pub ticketLen: int,
    /// The resumption PSK derived for the current ticket, and the ticket's
    /// `ticket_age_add` (the server's obfuscation offset for the age
    /// calculation).
    pub ticketPsk: ptr, pub ticketAgeAdd: long,
    /// The hash `kind` in force when the ticket was issued (a resumed
    /// handshake must reuse the same hash), and whether a usable ticket exists.
    pub ticketKind: int, pub haveTicket: bool,
    /// Whether this handshake is offering a PSK, and whether the server
    /// accepted it (`pre_shared_key` echoed back). If not accepted the client
    /// falls back to the full ECDHE secret.
    pub offerPsk: bool, pub pskAccepted: bool,
    /// The 0-RTT early-data record key, IV and sequence counter, derived from
    /// the PSK before the ServerHello arrives.
    pub eKey: ptr, pub eIv: ptr, pub eSeq: long,
    /// Whether early data is being offered, and whether the server accepted it
    /// (signalled in EncryptedExtensions).
    pub offerEarly: bool, pub earlyAccepted: bool,
    /// The maximum plaintext record size the peer will accept, from the
    /// record_size_limit extension. Defaults to 16385 (the RFC 8446 maximum
    /// plus one for the content type).
    pub sendLimit: int,
    /// Set true when the server sent a CertificateRequest, meaning the client
    /// must respond with a Certificate (possibly empty) and CertificateVerify.
    pub certRequested: bool,
    /// Whether the client actually holds a certificate to present (set by
    /// [`setClientCert`]); if false an empty Certificate is sent.
    pub haveClientCert: bool,
    /// The client certificate DER to present and its length. `clientCertDer` is
    /// borrowed from the caller (stored, not copied), so it must outlive the
    /// handshake.
    pub clientCertDer: ptr, pub clientCertLen: int,
    /// The client's private signing key (a 32-byte P-256 scalar) for the client
    /// CertificateVerify. Copied into an owned buffer by [`setClientCert`].
    pub clientKey: ptr,
    /// Offset and length, within `hsBuf`, of a stapled OCSP response found in
    /// the leaf certificate's status_request entry extension. Zero length means
    /// none was stapled.
    pub ocspOff: int, pub ocspLen: int,
}

/// Constructs a fresh [`TlsClient`] for a full (non-resumption) handshake.
///
/// The three arguments are the caller's ephemeral private keys for X25519,
/// P-256 and P-384; they are stored by reference and must stay alive for the
/// handshake. Every owned scratch buffer is allocated here up front (transcript
/// 4096 bytes, handshake-reassembly 8192, plus the fixed-size secret/key/IV
/// buffers) so the hot path never has to check for a null buffer. Placeholder
/// one-byte buffers are used for `hrrCookie`, `ticket`, `clientCertDer` and so
/// on, and are reallocated to the real size when their data arrives.
pub fn newClient(ephPriv: ptr, ephPrivP256: ptr, ephPrivP384: ptr): TlsClient {
    return TlsClient {
        kind: 0, suite: 0, eph: ephPriv, ephP256: ephPrivP256, ephP384: ephPrivP384, trBuf: bytes.alloc(4096), trLen: 0, trCap: 4096,
        hs: bytes.alloc(48), chs: bytes.alloc(48), shs: bytes.alloc(48),
        cKey: bytes.alloc(32), cIv: bytes.alloc(12), sKey: bytes.alloc(32), sIv: bytes.alloc(12),
        master: bytes.alloc(48), cap: bytes.alloc(48), sap: bytes.alloc(48),
        capKey: bytes.alloc(32), capIv: bytes.alloc(12), sapKey: bytes.alloc(32), sapIv: bytes.alloc(12),
        sSeq: 0, cSeq: 0, certVerifyOk: false, finishedOk: false,
        hsBuf: bytes.alloc(8192), hsCap: 8192, hsLen: 0, hsPos: 0, leafOff: 0, leafLen: 0,
        certListOff: 0, certListLen: 0, alpnSel: "", hrrCookie: bytes.alloc(1), hrrCookieLen: 0,
        resMaster: bytes.alloc(48), ticket: bytes.alloc(1), ticketLen: 0, ticketPsk: bytes.alloc(48),
        ticketAgeAdd: 0, ticketKind: 0, haveTicket: false, offerPsk: false, pskAccepted: false,
        eKey: bytes.alloc(32), eIv: bytes.alloc(12), eSeq: 0, offerEarly: false, earlyAccepted: false,
        sendLimit: 16385,
        certRequested: false, haveClientCert: false, clientCertDer: bytes.alloc(1), clientCertLen: 0, clientKey: bytes.alloc(32),
        ocspOff: 0, ocspLen: 0,
    };
}

/// A located OCSP staple: byte offset and length of the DER OCSP response
/// inside some certificate-entry extension buffer.
///
/// Returned by [`findOcspStaple`]. An all-zero value (off 0, len 0) means no
/// staple was present.
pub struct OcspStaple {
    /// Offset of the first byte of the OCSP response, relative to the buffer
    /// that was scanned.
    pub off: int,
    /// Length of the OCSP response in bytes; 0 when there is no staple.
    pub len: int,
    /// Wraps an offset/length pair.
    init(off: int, len: int) { self.off = off; self.len = len; }
}

/// Scans a certificate-entry extension block for a stapled OCSP response.
///
/// Walks the extensions in `[extOff, extOff+extLen)` looking for a
/// status_request extension (type 0x0005) whose body is a
/// CertificateStatus of type ocsp (leading byte 1). When found it returns the
/// offset and length of the inner OCSP response (skipping the 1-byte status
/// type and 3-byte length). Returns an empty [`OcspStaple`] if none is present.
/// Used while parsing the server Certificate to surface a stapled response the
/// caller can check without a separate OCSP round trip.
pub fn findOcspStaple(buf: ptr, extOff: int, extLen: int): OcspStaple {
    let p = extOff;
    let end = extOff + extLen;
    while (p + 4 <= end) {
        let etype = (bytes.read_byte(buf, p) << 8) | bytes.read_byte(buf, p + 1);
        let elen = (bytes.read_byte(buf, p + 2) << 8) | bytes.read_byte(buf, p + 3);
        if (etype == 0x0005) {
            let cs = p + 4;
            if (elen >= 4 && bytes.read_byte(buf, cs) == 1) {
                let rlen = (bytes.read_byte(buf, cs + 1) << 16) | (bytes.read_byte(buf, cs + 2) << 8) | bytes.read_byte(buf, cs + 3);
                return OcspStaple(cs + 4, rlen);
            }
        }
        p = p + 4 + elen;
    }
    return OcspStaple(0, 0);
}

/// Registers a client certificate and its signing key for mutual TLS.
///
/// `certDer` is stored by reference (borrowed, not copied), so it must outlive
/// the handshake; the 32-byte P-256 signing key is copied into the owned
/// `clientKey` buffer. Sets `haveClientCert` so the client responds to a
/// CertificateRequest with this certificate and a real CertificateVerify rather
/// than an empty Certificate. See [`buildClientCertFlightRecord`].
pub fn setClientCert(self: TlsClient, certDer: ptr, certLen: int, key: ptr): void {
    self.clientCertDer = certDer;
    self.clientCertLen = certLen;
    let i = 0;
    while (i < 32) { bytes.write_byte(self.clientKey, i, bytes.read_byte(key, i)); i = i + 1; }
    self.haveClientCert = true;
}

/// Reports whether the server sent a CertificateRequest during the handshake.
///
/// A `true` result means the caller should send the client certificate flight
/// with [`buildClientCertFlightRecord`] before its Finished.
pub fn wasCertRequested(self: TlsClient): bool { return self.certRequested; }

/// Builds and encrypts the client's certificate flight into `out`, returning
/// the record length.
///
/// Emits the client Certificate message (the real certificate if
/// `haveClientCert`, otherwise an empty one) and, only when a certificate is
/// present, a CertificateVerify signed with `clientKey` over the current
/// transcript hash. Both inner messages are appended to the transcript via
/// [`trAdd`] before the flight is AEAD-protected as a single handshake record
/// with the client handshake keys, and `cSeq` is advanced. The scratch `inner`
/// buffer is sized for the certificate plus headroom and freed before return.
pub fn buildClientCertFlightRecord(self: TlsClient, out: ptr): int {
    let hl = trHashLen(self);
    let inner = bytes.alloc(4 + self.clientCertLen + 256);
    let certMsgLen = buildClientCertificate(if (self.haveClientCert) self.clientCertDer else inner,
                                            if (self.haveClientCert) self.clientCertLen else 0, inner);
    trAdd(self, inner, certMsgLen);
    let innerLen = certMsgLen;
    if (self.haveClientCert) {
        let hCert = bytes.alloc(48); trHash(self, hCert);
        let cvPtr = (inner as long + certMsgLen) as ptr;
        let cvLen = buildClientCertVerify(hCert, hl, self.clientKey, cvPtr);
        trAdd(self, cvPtr, cvLen);
        bytes.free(hCert);
        innerLen = certMsgLen + cvLen;
    }
    let n = tls.recordProtect(self.suite, self.cKey, self.cIv, self.cSeq, inner, innerLen, 0x16, out);
    self.cSeq = self.cSeq + 1;
    bytes.free(inner);
    return n;
}

/// Constructs a [`TlsClient`] primed to resume a session with a PSK.
///
/// Starts from a normal [`newClient`] and then enables the resumption path:
/// `offerPsk` and `haveTicket` are set, the hash `kind` is taken from the
/// ticket (a resumed handshake must reuse the issuing suite's hash), and the
/// PSK and ticket bytes are copied into freshly sized owned buffers (the
/// placeholder buffers from `newClient` are freed first). `ageAdd` is the
/// ticket's `ticket_age_add`, needed to compute the obfuscated age offered in
/// the pre_shared_key extension.
pub fn newClientResume(ephPriv: ptr, ephPrivP256: ptr, ephPrivP384: ptr, psk: ptr, pskLen: int, kind: int,
                       ticket: ptr, ticketLen: int, ageAdd: long): TlsClient {
    let c = newClient(ephPriv, ephPrivP256, ephPrivP384);
    c.offerPsk = true;
    c.haveTicket = true;
    c.ticketKind = kind;
    c.kind = kind;
    bytes.free(c.ticketPsk); c.ticketPsk = bytes.alloc(pskLen);
    let i = 0;
    while (i < pskLen) { bytes.write_byte(c.ticketPsk, i, bytes.read_byte(psk, i)); i = i + 1; }
    bytes.free(c.ticket); c.ticket = bytes.alloc(ticketLen);
    i = 0;
    while (i < ticketLen) { bytes.write_byte(c.ticket, i, bytes.read_byte(ticket, i)); i = i + 1; }
    c.ticketLen = ticketLen;
    c.ticketAgeAdd = ageAdd;
    return c;
}

/// Processes a HelloRetryRequest and returns the group the server wants the
/// client to retry with.
///
/// Parses the HRR to pick up the negotiated cipher suite (setting `suite` and
/// `kind`), the requested key_share group (extension 0x0033), and any cookie
/// (extension 0x002c) which is copied into an owned `hrrCookie` for replay.
///
/// Crucially it rewrites the transcript to the synthetic "message_hash" form
/// that RFC 8446 section 4.4.1 requires after an HRR: the original ClientHello
/// is replaced by a `message_hash` message (type 0xfe) wrapping its hash, then
/// the HRR itself is appended. This keeps the transcript consistent so the
/// retried ClientHello and all later secrets bind correctly. Returns the group
/// code (for example 0x0017 for P-256, 0x0018 for P-384) so the caller can
/// build the retry with the right key share.
pub fn processHelloRetryRequest(self: TlsClient, hrr: ptr, hrrLen: int): int {
    let p = 4 + 2 + 32;
    let sidLen = bytes.read_byte(hrr, p);
    p = p + 1 + sidLen;
    let cipher = (bytes.read_byte(hrr, p) << 8) | bytes.read_byte(hrr, p + 1);
    p = p + 2 + 1;
    if (cipher == 0x1302) { self.suite = 1; self.kind = 1; }
    else if (cipher == 0x1303) { self.suite = 2; self.kind = 0; }
    else { self.suite = 0; self.kind = 0; }
    let extLen = (bytes.read_byte(hrr, p) << 8) | bytes.read_byte(hrr, p + 1);
    p = p + 2;
    let end = p + extLen;
    let group = 0;
    while (p + 4 <= end) {
        let etype = (bytes.read_byte(hrr, p) << 8) | bytes.read_byte(hrr, p + 1);
        let elen = (bytes.read_byte(hrr, p + 2) << 8) | bytes.read_byte(hrr, p + 3);
        let body = p + 4;
        if (etype == 0x0033) {
            group = (bytes.read_byte(hrr, body) << 8) | bytes.read_byte(hrr, body + 1);
        } else if (etype == 0x002c) {
            let clen = (bytes.read_byte(hrr, body) << 8) | bytes.read_byte(hrr, body + 1);
            bytes.free(self.hrrCookie);
            self.hrrCookie = bytes.alloc(clen);
            let k = 0;
            while (k < clen) { bytes.write_byte(self.hrrCookie, k, bytes.read_byte(hrr, body + 2 + k)); k = k + 1; }
            self.hrrCookieLen = clen;
        }
        p = body + elen;
    }
    let hl = trHashLen(self);
    let h1 = bytes.alloc(hl);
    trHash(self, h1);
    self.trLen = 0;
    let mh = bytes.alloc(4 + hl);
    bytes.write_byte(mh, 0, 0xfe);
    bytes.write_byte(mh, 1, 0); bytes.write_byte(mh, 2, 0); bytes.write_byte(mh, 3, hl);
    let k = 0;
    while (k < hl) { bytes.write_byte(mh, 4 + k, bytes.read_byte(h1, k)); k = k + 1; }
    trAdd(self, mh, 4 + hl);
    trAdd(self, hrr, hrrLen);
    bytes.free(mh); bytes.free(h1);
    return group;
}

/// Pointer to the stored HelloRetryRequest cookie, for building the retry
/// ClientHello's cookie extension.
pub fn hrrCookiePtr(self: TlsClient): ptr { return self.hrrCookie; }
/// Length in bytes of the stored HelloRetryRequest cookie; 0 if there is none.
pub fn hrrCookieSize(self: TlsClient): int { return self.hrrCookieLen; }

/// Appends `n` bytes of a handshake message to the transcript buffer, growing
/// it if needed.
///
/// The transcript must contain every handshake message in wire order, since all
/// TLS 1.3 secrets and the Finished MAC hash it. When capacity is exceeded the
/// buffer is reallocated to twice the required size and the old contents copied
/// across. Every function that consumes or emits a handshake message calls this
/// exactly once for that message. See [`trHash`].
fn trAdd(self: TlsClient, msg: ptr, n: int): void {
    if (self.trLen + n > self.trCap) {
        let nc = (self.trLen + n) * 2;
        let nb = bytes.alloc(nc);
        let i = 0;
        while (i < self.trLen) { bytes.write_byte(nb, i, bytes.read_byte(self.trBuf, i)); i = i + 1; }
        bytes.free(self.trBuf);
        self.trBuf = nb;
        self.trCap = nc;
    }
    let i = 0;
    while (i < n) { bytes.write_byte(self.trBuf, self.trLen + i, bytes.read_byte(msg, i)); i = i + 1; }
    self.trLen = self.trLen + n;
}
/// Writes the running transcript hash into `out`.
///
/// Hashes the first `trLen` bytes of the transcript with the negotiated hash:
/// SHA-384 when `kind == 1`, otherwise SHA-256. `out` must have room for the
/// digest ([`trHashLen`] bytes). Called at every point that mixes the
/// transcript into the key schedule.
fn trHash(self: TlsClient, out: ptr): void {
    if (self.kind == 1) { sha512.hash384Into(self.trBuf, self.trLen, out); }
    else { sha256.hashInto(self.trBuf, self.trLen, out); }
}
/// Digest length in bytes for the negotiated hash: 48 for SHA-384 (`kind == 1`),
/// 32 for SHA-256.
fn trHashLen(self: TlsClient): int { if (self.kind == 1) { return 48; } return 32; }

/// Records the client's own ClientHello into the transcript.
///
/// Called by the connection layer right after it builds and sends a
/// ClientHello, since the client is the origin of that message and it must be
/// the first entry in the transcript. Thin wrapper over [`trAdd`].
pub fn addClientHello(self: TlsClient, chMsg: ptr, chLen: int): void {
    trAdd(self, chMsg, chLen);
}

/// Processes the ServerHello: performs the ECDHE and derives the handshake
/// secrets and keys.
///
/// Parses the server's chosen cipher suite and key_share to set `suite`/`kind`
/// and recover the server's public share, then runs the ECDHE against the
/// matching ephemeral private key (P-384 for group 0x0018, P-256 for 0x0017,
/// X25519 otherwise). The early secret comes from the PSK when one was offered
/// and the server accepted it (setting `pskAccepted`), otherwise from the
/// all-zero early secret. From Derive-Secret and Extract it produces the
/// handshake secret `hs`, then appends the ServerHello to the transcript and
/// derives the client and server handshake-traffic secrets and their record
/// keys and IVs. After this returns the client can decrypt the server's
/// handshake flight. All scratch buffers are freed before return.
pub fn processServerHello(self: TlsClient, shMsg: ptr, shLen: int): void {
    let serverPub = bytes.alloc(97);
    let groupBuf = bytes.alloc(2);
    let cipher = handshake.parseServerHello(shMsg, shLen, serverPub, groupBuf);
    let group = (bytes.read_byte(groupBuf, 0) << 8) | bytes.read_byte(groupBuf, 1);
    if (cipher == 0x1302) { self.suite = 1; self.kind = 1; }
    else if (cipher == 0x1303) { self.suite = 2; self.kind = 0; }
    else { self.suite = 0; self.kind = 0; }

    let ecdhe = bytes.alloc(48);
    let ecdheLen = 32;
    if (group == 0x0018) { p384.ecdh(ecdhe, self.ephP384, serverPub); ecdheLen = 48; }
    else if (group == 0x0017) { p256.ecdh(ecdhe, self.ephP256, serverPub); }
    else { x25519.scalarMult(ecdhe, self.eph, serverPub); }
    let early = bytes.alloc(48);
    if (self.offerPsk && handshake.serverAcceptedPsk(shMsg, shLen)) {
        self.pskAccepted = true;
        tls.earlySecretPsk(self.kind, self.ticketPsk, trHashLen(self), early);
    } else {
        tls.earlySecret(self.kind, early);
    }
    let derived = bytes.alloc(48); tls.deriveDerived(self.kind, early, derived);
    tls.extractSecret(self.kind, derived, ecdhe, ecdheLen, self.hs);

    trAdd(self, shMsg, shLen);
    let hSH = bytes.alloc(48); trHash(self, hSH);
    tls.deriveTraffic(self.kind, self.hs, "c hs traffic", hSH, self.chs);
    tls.deriveTraffic(self.kind, self.hs, "s hs traffic", hSH, self.shs);
    tls.deriveKeyIv(self.kind, self.suite, self.chs, self.cKey, self.cIv);
    tls.deriveKeyIv(self.kind, self.suite, self.shs, self.sKey, self.sIv);
    bytes.free(serverPub); bytes.free(groupBuf); bytes.free(ecdhe); bytes.free(early); bytes.free(derived); bytes.free(hSH);
}

/// Unprotects one server handshake record and processes every handshake
/// message inside it, returning whether the peer is authenticated.
///
/// This is the single-record path (contrast the streaming [`feedHandshake`]).
/// It decrypts `record` with the server handshake keys, advances `sSeq`, and
/// walks the contained handshake messages, dispatching on type:
/// Certificate (0x0b) records the leaf position, CertificateVerify (0x0f)
/// verifies the server's signature over the transcript and sets `certVerifyOk`,
/// Finished (0x14) verifies the server Finished MAC and sets `finishedOk`, and
/// EncryptedExtensions (0x08) picks up the selected ALPN, early-data acceptance
/// and record_size_limit. Every message is folded into the transcript in order.
/// Returns `certVerifyOk && finishedOk`, that is, true only when both the
/// certificate signature and the Finished MAC checked out. Returns false if the
/// record fails to decrypt.
///
/// Note this path expects the leaf certificate to be readable directly from the
/// decrypted buffer; the streaming [`feedHandshake`] is what the connection
/// layer normally uses because it survives records that split a message.
pub fn processFlight(self: TlsClient, record: ptr, recLen: int): bool {
    let inner = bytes.alloc(recLen);
    let innerLen = tls.recordUnprotect(self.suite, self.sKey, self.sIv, self.sSeq, record, recLen, inner);
    self.sSeq = self.sSeq + 1;
    if (innerLen < 0) { bytes.free(inner); return false; }
    let typeOut = bytes.alloc(1);
    let contentLen = tls.innerParse(inner, innerLen, typeOut);
    bytes.free(typeOut);

    let leafBuf: ptr = inner;
    let leafLen = 0;
    let p = 0;
    while (p < contentLen) {
        let mtype = bytes.read_byte(inner, p);
        let mlen = (bytes.read_byte(inner, p + 1) << 16) | (bytes.read_byte(inner, p + 2) << 8)
                 | bytes.read_byte(inner, p + 3);
        let msgLen = 4 + mlen;
        if (mtype == 0x0b) {
            let clen = (bytes.read_byte(inner, p + 8) << 16) | (bytes.read_byte(inner, p + 9) << 8)
                     | bytes.read_byte(inner, p + 10);
            leafBuf = at(inner, p + 11);
            leafLen = clen;
            trAdd(self, at(inner, p), msgLen);
        } else if (mtype == 0x0f) {
            let hCert = bytes.alloc(48);
            trHash(self, hCert);
            let scheme = (bytes.read_byte(inner, p + 4) << 8) | bytes.read_byte(inner, p + 5);
            let siglen = (bytes.read_byte(inner, p + 6) << 8) | bytes.read_byte(inner, p + 7);
            let cert = x509.parseCert(leafBuf, leafLen);
            self.certVerifyOk = handshake.verifyCertificateVerify(cert, scheme, at(inner, p + 8), siglen, hCert, trHashLen(self));
            bytes.free(hCert);
            trAdd(self, at(inner, p), msgLen);
        } else if (mtype == 0x14) {
            let hCV = bytes.alloc(48);
            trHash(self, hCV);
            self.finishedOk = handshake.verifyFinished(self.kind, self.shs, hCV, at(inner, p + 4));
            bytes.free(hCV);
            trAdd(self, at(inner, p), msgLen);
        } else if (mtype == 0x08) {
            self.alpnSel = handshake.parseAlpnSelection(inner, p);
            if (self.offerEarly) { self.earlyAccepted = handshake.serverAcceptedEarlyData(inner, p); }
            let rsl1 = handshake.parseRecordSizeLimit(inner, p); if (rsl1 > 0) { self.sendLimit = rsl1; }
            trAdd(self, at(inner, p), msgLen);
        } else {
            trAdd(self, at(inner, p), msgLen);
        }
        p = p + msgLen;
    }
    bytes.free(inner);
    return self.certVerifyOk && self.finishedOk;
}

/// Appends `n` decrypted handshake bytes to the streaming reassembly buffer
/// `hsBuf`, growing it if needed.
///
/// [`feedHandshake`] uses this to stitch together handshake messages that
/// arrive across multiple records. Mirrors [`trAdd`]'s doubling growth
/// strategy, but targets `hsBuf`/`hsLen`/`hsCap` (the reassembly area) rather
/// than the transcript.
fn hsAppend(self: TlsClient, src: ptr, n: int): void {
    if (self.hsLen + n > self.hsCap) {
        let nc = (self.hsLen + n) * 2;
        let nb = bytes.alloc(nc);
        let i = 0;
        while (i < self.hsLen) { bytes.write_byte(nb, i, bytes.read_byte(self.hsBuf, i)); i = i + 1; }
        bytes.free(self.hsBuf);
        self.hsBuf = nb;
        self.hsCap = nc;
    }
    let i = 0;
    while (i < n) { bytes.write_byte(self.hsBuf, self.hsLen + i, bytes.read_byte(src, i)); i = i + 1; }
    self.hsLen = self.hsLen + n;
}

/// Streaming server-flight processor: unprotects one record, buffers its
/// handshake bytes, and processes every complete message now available.
///
/// This is the path the connection layer drives during the server flight, and
/// it is robust to records that split a handshake message or pack several
/// together. It ignores non-application-data records (only `rtype == 0x17`
/// carries protected handshake data here), decrypts, and appends the handshake
/// content to `hsBuf` via [`hsAppend`]. It then consumes as many whole messages
/// from `hsPos` as are present, handling: Certificate (0x0b), which records the
/// certificate-list and leaf offsets and locates any OCSP staple;
/// CertificateVerify (0x0f), which verifies the server signature and sets
/// `certVerifyOk`; Finished (0x14), which verifies the server MAC, sets
/// `finishedOk` and marks the flight done; EncryptedExtensions (0x08), which
/// reads ALPN, early-data acceptance and record_size_limit; and
/// CertificateRequest (0x0d), which sets `certRequested`. Each message is added
/// to the transcript.
///
/// Returns 1 once the server Finished has been processed, 0 if more records are
/// still needed, or -1 if a record failed to decrypt. A partial trailing
/// message leaves `hsPos` where it is so the next call resumes cleanly.
pub fn feedHandshake(self: TlsClient, record: ptr, recLen: int): int {
    let rtype = bytes.read_byte(record, 0);
    if (rtype != 0x17) { return 0; }
    let inner = bytes.alloc(recLen);
    let innerLen = tls.recordUnprotect(self.suite, self.sKey, self.sIv, self.sSeq, record, recLen, inner);
    self.sSeq = self.sSeq + 1;
    if (innerLen < 0) { bytes.free(inner); return -1; }
    let typeOut = bytes.alloc(1);
    let contentLen = tls.innerParse(inner, innerLen, typeOut);
    let ctype = bytes.read_byte(typeOut, 0);
    bytes.free(typeOut);
    if (ctype == 0x16) { hsAppend(self, inner, contentLen); }
    bytes.free(inner);

    let done = 0;
    while (self.hsPos + 4 <= self.hsLen) {
        let mlen = (bytes.read_byte(self.hsBuf, self.hsPos + 1) << 16)
                 | (bytes.read_byte(self.hsBuf, self.hsPos + 2) << 8)
                 | bytes.read_byte(self.hsBuf, self.hsPos + 3);
        let msgLen = 4 + mlen;
        if (self.hsPos + msgLen > self.hsLen) { return done; }
        let mtype = bytes.read_byte(self.hsBuf, self.hsPos);
        let mp = self.hsPos;
        if (mtype == 0x0b) {
            self.certListLen = (bytes.read_byte(self.hsBuf, mp + 5) << 16) | (bytes.read_byte(self.hsBuf, mp + 6) << 8)
                             | bytes.read_byte(self.hsBuf, mp + 7);
            self.certListOff = mp + 8;
            let clen = (bytes.read_byte(self.hsBuf, mp + 8) << 16) | (bytes.read_byte(self.hsBuf, mp + 9) << 8)
                     | bytes.read_byte(self.hsBuf, mp + 10);
            self.leafOff = mp + 11;
            self.leafLen = clen;
            let entryExtPos = self.leafOff + self.leafLen;
            let entryExtLen = (bytes.read_byte(self.hsBuf, entryExtPos) << 8) | bytes.read_byte(self.hsBuf, entryExtPos + 1);
            let staple = findOcspStaple(self.hsBuf, entryExtPos + 2, entryExtLen);
            self.ocspOff = staple.off;
            self.ocspLen = staple.len;
            trAdd(self, at(self.hsBuf, mp), msgLen);
        } else if (mtype == 0x0f) {
            let hCert = bytes.alloc(48);
            trHash(self, hCert);
            let scheme = (bytes.read_byte(self.hsBuf, mp + 4) << 8) | bytes.read_byte(self.hsBuf, mp + 5);
            let siglen = (bytes.read_byte(self.hsBuf, mp + 6) << 8) | bytes.read_byte(self.hsBuf, mp + 7);
            let cert = x509.parseCert(at(self.hsBuf, self.leafOff), self.leafLen);
            self.certVerifyOk = handshake.verifyCertificateVerify(cert, scheme, at(self.hsBuf, mp + 8), siglen, hCert, trHashLen(self));
            bytes.free(hCert);
            trAdd(self, at(self.hsBuf, mp), msgLen);
        } else if (mtype == 0x14) {
            let hCV = bytes.alloc(48);
            trHash(self, hCV);
            self.finishedOk = handshake.verifyFinished(self.kind, self.shs, hCV, at(self.hsBuf, mp + 4));
            bytes.free(hCV);
            trAdd(self, at(self.hsBuf, mp), msgLen);
            done = 1;
        } else if (mtype == 0x08) {
            self.alpnSel = handshake.parseAlpnSelection(self.hsBuf, mp);
            if (self.offerEarly) { self.earlyAccepted = handshake.serverAcceptedEarlyData(self.hsBuf, mp); }
            let rsl2 = handshake.parseRecordSizeLimit(self.hsBuf, mp); if (rsl2 > 0) { self.sendLimit = rsl2; }
            trAdd(self, at(self.hsBuf, mp), msgLen);
        } else if (mtype == 0x0d) {
            self.certRequested = true;
            trAdd(self, at(self.hsBuf, mp), msgLen);
        } else {
            trAdd(self, at(self.hsBuf, mp), msgLen);
        }
        self.hsPos = self.hsPos + msgLen;
    }
    return done;
}

/// Derives the application-phase traffic secrets, keys and IVs.
///
/// Called once the server Finished has been verified. It hashes the transcript
/// up to and including the server Finished, computes the master secret from the
/// handshake secret, then derives the client and server application traffic
/// secrets ("c ap traffic" / "s ap traffic") and expands each into its record
/// key and IV. After this the connection can send and receive protected
/// application data with [`encryptApp`] and [`decryptApp`].
pub fn deriveAppSecrets(self: TlsClient): void {
    let hSF = bytes.alloc(48);
    trHash(self, hSF);
    handshake.masterSecret(self.kind, self.hs, self.master);
    tls.deriveTraffic(self.kind, self.master, "c ap traffic", hSF, self.cap);
    tls.deriveTraffic(self.kind, self.master, "s ap traffic", hSF, self.sap);
    tls.deriveKeyIv(self.kind, self.suite, self.cap, self.capKey, self.capIv);
    tls.deriveKeyIv(self.kind, self.suite, self.sap, self.sapKey, self.sapIv);
    bytes.free(hSF);
}

/// Computes the client Finished verify data, folds the Finished into the
/// transcript, and derives the resumption master secret.
///
/// Writes the client's Finished verify data (an HMAC over the transcript with
/// the client handshake finished key) into `vdOut`, then builds the full
/// Finished handshake message and appends it to the transcript via [`trAdd`] so
/// the transcript now ends at "client Finished". From that final transcript
/// hash it derives `resMaster`, the resumption master secret, which later backs
/// any NewSessionTicket PSK. Pair this with [`clientFinishedRecord`], which
/// produces the actual protected record from the same `vdOut`.
pub fn clientFinished(self: TlsClient, vdOut: ptr): void {
    let hl = trHashLen(self);
    let hCtx = bytes.alloc(48);
    trHash(self, hCtx);
    handshake.finishedVerifyData(self.kind, self.chs, hCtx, vdOut);
    bytes.free(hCtx);

    let cf = bytes.alloc(4 + hl);
    bytes.write_byte(cf, 0, 0x14); bytes.write_byte(cf, 1, 0); bytes.write_byte(cf, 2, 0); bytes.write_byte(cf, 3, hl);
    let ci = 0;
    while (ci < hl) { bytes.write_byte(cf, 4 + ci, bytes.read_byte(vdOut, ci)); ci = ci + 1; }
    trAdd(self, cf, 4 + hl);
    let hCF = bytes.alloc(hl);
    trHash(self, hCF);
    tls.deriveResMaster(self.kind, self.master, hCF, self.resMaster);
    bytes.free(cf); bytes.free(hCF);
}

/// Builds and encrypts the client Finished record into `out`, returning its
/// length.
///
/// Wraps `verifyData` (the 32- or 48-byte value produced by [`clientFinished`])
/// in a Finished handshake message and AEAD-protects it with the client
/// handshake keys. It then resets BOTH sequence counters to 0, because sending
/// the client Finished is the point where the client switches to application
/// keys: the next records in each direction start a new nonce sequence.
pub fn clientFinishedRecord(self: TlsClient, verifyData: ptr, out: ptr): int {
    let hl = trHashLen(self);
    let msg = bytes.alloc(4 + hl);
    bytes.write_byte(msg, 0, 0x14);
    bytes.write_byte(msg, 1, 0);
    bytes.write_byte(msg, 2, 0);
    bytes.write_byte(msg, 3, hl);
    let i = 0;
    while (i < hl) { bytes.write_byte(msg, 4 + i, bytes.read_byte(verifyData, i)); i = i + 1; }
    let n = tls.recordProtect(self.suite, self.cKey, self.cIv, self.cSeq, msg, 4 + hl, 0x16, out);
    bytes.free(msg);
    self.cSeq = 0;
    self.sSeq = 0;
    return n;
}

/// Writes `v` as a big-endian 3-byte (24-bit) integer at `off`.
///
/// TLS uses 24-bit lengths in several places (the handshake message length, the
/// certificate_list length, individual certificate lengths), so this is the
/// shared encoder for those "uint24" fields.
fn w3c(out: ptr, off: int, v: int): void {
    bytes.write_byte(out, off, (v >> 16) & 0xff);
    bytes.write_byte(out, off + 1, (v >> 8) & 0xff);
    bytes.write_byte(out, off + 2, v & 0xff);
}

/// Builds a client Certificate handshake message into `out`, returning its
/// length.
///
/// Emits the TLS 1.3 Certificate structure: a 1-byte empty
/// certificate_request_context, then the certificate_list. When `certLen > 0`
/// the list holds one CertificateEntry (3-byte length, the DER, and a 2-byte
/// empty entry-extensions field); when `certLen` is 0 the list is empty, which
/// is the valid "I have no certificate" response to a CertificateRequest. All
/// the 24-bit lengths are written with [`w3c`].
pub fn buildClientCertificate(certDer: ptr, certLen: int, out: ptr): int {
    let listLen = if (certLen > 0) 3 + certLen + 2 else 0;
    let bodyLen = 1 + 3 + listLen;
    let p = 0;
    bytes.write_byte(out, p, 0x0b); p = p + 1;
    w3c(out, p, bodyLen); p = p + 3;
    bytes.write_byte(out, p, 0); p = p + 1;
    w3c(out, p, listLen); p = p + 3;
    if (certLen > 0) {
        w3c(out, p, certLen); p = p + 3;
        let i = 0;
        while (i < certLen) { bytes.write_byte(out, p + i, bytes.read_byte(certDer, i)); i = i + 1; }
        p = p + certLen;
        bytes.write_byte(out, p, 0); bytes.write_byte(out, p + 1, 0); p = p + 2;
    }
    return p;
}

/// Builds a client CertificateVerify handshake message into `out`, returning
/// its length.
///
/// Signs the TLS 1.3 CertificateVerify content (the fixed context string plus
/// `transcriptHash`, assembled by `handshake.certVerifyDigest` with the client
/// flag false) using ECDSA P-256 with the client key, DER-encodes the (r, s)
/// signature, and wraps it with the ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256 scheme code
/// (0x0403) and a 2-byte signature length. `hl` is the transcript hash length.
/// All scratch buffers (digest, r, s, DER signature) are freed before return.
pub fn buildClientCertVerify(transcriptHash: ptr, hl: int, key: ptr, out: ptr): int {
    let digest = bytes.alloc(32);
    handshake.certVerifyDigest(false, transcriptHash, hl, digest);
    let r = bytes.alloc(32); let s = bytes.alloc(32);
    p256.ecdsaSign(digest, key, r, s);
    let sigDer = bytes.alloc(80);
    let sigLen = handshake.encodeEcdsaSigDer(r, s, sigDer);
    let bodyLen = 4 + sigLen;
    let p = 0;
    bytes.write_byte(out, p, 0x0f); p = p + 1;
    w3c(out, p, bodyLen); p = p + 3;
    bytes.write_byte(out, p, 0x04); bytes.write_byte(out, p + 1, 0x03); p = p + 2;
    bytes.write_byte(out, p, (sigLen >> 8) & 0xff); bytes.write_byte(out, p + 1, sigLen & 0xff); p = p + 2;
    let i = 0;
    while (i < sigLen) { bytes.write_byte(out, p + i, bytes.read_byte(sigDer, i)); i = i + 1; }
    p = p + sigLen;
    bytes.free(digest); bytes.free(r); bytes.free(s); bytes.free(sigDer);
    return p;
}

/// Encrypts `dataLen` bytes of application data into a protected record in
/// `out`, returning the record length.
///
/// AEAD-protects the plaintext as an application_data record (content type
/// 0x17) with the client application key and IV, then advances `cSeq`. This is
/// the send path for ordinary post-handshake traffic. The caller must keep
/// `dataLen` within the negotiated `sendLimit`.
pub fn encryptApp(self: TlsClient, data: ptr, dataLen: int, out: ptr): int {
    let n = tls.recordProtect(self.suite, self.capKey, self.capIv, self.cSeq, data, dataLen, 0x17, out);
    self.cSeq = self.cSeq + 1;
    return n;
}

/// Encrypts a two-byte TLS alert (level, description) into a protected record
/// in `out`, returning the record length.
///
/// Used to send a close_notify or a fatal alert once application keys are in
/// force. The alert is protected as an alert record (content type 0x15) with
/// the client application key, and `cSeq` is advanced. The scratch alert buffer
/// is freed before return.
pub fn encryptAlert(self: TlsClient, level: int, desc: int, out: ptr): int {
    let a = bytes.alloc(2);
    bytes.write_byte(a, 0, level); bytes.write_byte(a, 1, desc);
    let n = tls.recordProtect(self.suite, self.capKey, self.capIv, self.cSeq, a, 2, 0x15, out);
    self.cSeq = self.cSeq + 1;
    bytes.free(a);
    return n;
}


/// Rotates the server application traffic secret and keys after a server
/// KeyUpdate.
///
/// Applies the "traffic secret update" HKDF step to `sap` in place, re-derives
/// the server application key and IV from the new secret, and resets `sSeq` to
/// 0 (a key change restarts the nonce sequence). Call this when the server
/// sends a KeyUpdate so subsequent inbound records decrypt with the new keys.
pub fn updateServerKey(self: TlsClient): void {
    let ns = bytes.alloc(48);
    tls.deriveTrafficUpdate(self.kind, self.sap, ns);
    let i = 0;
    while (i < 48) { bytes.write_byte(self.sap, i, bytes.read_byte(ns, i)); i = i + 1; }
    bytes.free(ns);
    tls.deriveKeyIv(self.kind, self.suite, self.sap, self.sapKey, self.sapIv);
    self.sSeq = 0;
}

/// Rotates the client application traffic secret and keys for a client-side
/// KeyUpdate.
///
/// Mirror of [`updateServerKey`] for the send direction: advances `cap`
/// in place, re-derives the client application key and IV, and resets `cSeq`.
/// Call this after emitting a KeyUpdate with [`keyUpdateRecord`] so outbound
/// records use the new keys.
pub fn updateClientKey(self: TlsClient): void {
    let ns = bytes.alloc(48);
    tls.deriveTrafficUpdate(self.kind, self.cap, ns);
    let i = 0;
    while (i < 48) { bytes.write_byte(self.cap, i, bytes.read_byte(ns, i)); i = i + 1; }
    bytes.free(ns);
    tls.deriveKeyIv(self.kind, self.suite, self.cap, self.capKey, self.capIv);
    self.cSeq = 0;
}

/// Builds and encrypts a KeyUpdate handshake record into `out`, returning its
/// length.
///
/// `request` is the KeyUpdateRequest byte: 1 (update_requested) asks the peer
/// to update its own keys and reply with a KeyUpdate, 0 (update_not_requested)
/// does not. The message is AEAD-protected with the current client application
/// key and `cSeq` is advanced. Note this only SENDS the KeyUpdate; the caller
/// must then call [`updateClientKey`] to actually rotate the send keys.
pub fn keyUpdateRecord(self: TlsClient, request: int, out: ptr): int {
    let msg = bytes.alloc(5);
    bytes.write_byte(msg, 0, 0x18);
    bytes.write_byte(msg, 1, 0); bytes.write_byte(msg, 2, 0); bytes.write_byte(msg, 3, 1);
    bytes.write_byte(msg, 4, request);
    let n = tls.recordProtect(self.suite, self.capKey, self.capIv, self.cSeq, msg, 5, 0x16, out);
    self.cSeq = self.cSeq + 1;
    bytes.free(msg);
    return n;
}

/// Decrypts a protected server application record into `out`, returning the
/// plaintext length (or a negative value on failure).
///
/// Unprotects `record` with the server application key and IV and advances
/// `sSeq`. This is the receive counterpart to [`encryptApp`]. The returned
/// length includes only the inner plaintext; the caller inspects the trailing
/// content type to distinguish application_data from an alert or a
/// post-handshake message.
pub fn decryptApp(self: TlsClient, record: ptr, recLen: int, out: ptr): int {
    let n = tls.recordUnprotect(self.suite, self.sapKey, self.sapIv, self.sSeq, record, recLen, out);
    self.sSeq = self.sSeq + 1;
    return n;
}

/// Verifies the server's certificate chain against a trust anchor, returning
/// whether it is valid for `host` at time `now`.
///
/// Runs full X.509 path validation over the stored certificate_list (built up
/// during [`feedHandshake`]) against the given root DER: signature chaining,
/// validity window against `now`, and hostname matching against `host`. Returns
/// false immediately if no certificate list was captured. This is separate from
/// the CertificateVerify check ([`feedHandshake`] sets `certVerifyOk`): that
/// proves the peer holds the leaf's private key, this proves the leaf is
/// trusted for the name. Both must pass for a secure connection.
pub fn verifyServerChain(self: TlsClient, rootDer: ptr, rootLen: int, host: string, now: long): bool {
    if (self.certListLen <= 0) { return false; }
    return x509.verifyCertList(at(self.hsBuf, self.certListOff), self.certListLen, rootDer, rootLen, host, now);
}

/// Pointer to the raw server certificate_list body inside `hsBuf`.
///
/// Valid only after the Certificate message has been parsed. The address is
/// computed in `long` to avoid truncation. See [`verifyServerChain`], which
/// consumes the same region.
pub fn certListPtr(self: TlsClient): ptr { return (self.hsBuf as long + self.certListOff) as ptr; }
/// Length in bytes of the server certificate_list body; 0 if none was seen.
pub fn certListSize(self: TlsClient): int { return self.certListLen; }
/// Pointer to the stapled OCSP response inside `hsBuf`, if any.
///
/// Meaningful only when [`ocspStapleSize`] is non-zero. Located by
/// [`findOcspStaple`] during Certificate parsing.
pub fn ocspStaplePtr(self: TlsClient): ptr { return (self.hsBuf as long + self.ocspOff) as ptr; }
/// Length in bytes of the stapled OCSP response; 0 when the server stapled none.
pub fn ocspStapleSize(self: TlsClient): int { return self.ocspLen; }

/// The ALPN protocol the server selected, or "" if ALPN was not negotiated.
pub fn selectedAlpn(self: TlsClient): string { return self.alpnSel; }

/// Writes the current transcript hash into `out` (needs [`trHashLen`] bytes).
///
/// Exposes the transcript digest to the connection layer, for example to derive
/// an exporter or channel-binding value outside this module.
pub fn transcriptHashInto(self: TlsClient, out: ptr): void { trHash(self, out); }


/// Computes the PSK binder and patches it into an already-built ClientHello.
///
/// The pre_shared_key binder is an HMAC that covers the ClientHello up to but
/// not including the binders themselves, so it can only be filled in after the
/// rest of the message exists (which is why [`buildClientHelloEx`] leaves zero
/// bytes there). `out` is the full ClientHello of length `chLen`. This hashes
/// the transcript concatenated with the truncated ClientHello (everything
/// before the binder list, that is `chLen - hl - 3` bytes of the message),
/// derives the binder key and finished key from the resumption early secret,
/// computes the binder HMAC, and writes it over the placeholder at the end of
/// the message. It uses SHA-384 or SHA-256 per `kind`. All scratch buffers are
/// freed before return.
pub fn fillResumptionBinder(self: TlsClient, out: ptr, chLen: int): void {
    let hl = trHashLen(self);
    let truncLen = chLen - hl - 3;
    let tmp = bytes.alloc(self.trLen + truncLen);
    let i = 0;
    while (i < self.trLen) { bytes.write_byte(tmp, i, bytes.read_byte(self.trBuf, i)); i = i + 1; }
    let j = 0;
    while (j < truncLen) { bytes.write_byte(tmp, self.trLen + j, bytes.read_byte(out, j)); j = j + 1; }
    let th = bytes.alloc(hl);
    if (self.kind == 1) { sha512.hash384Into(tmp, self.trLen + truncLen, th); }
    else { sha256.hashInto(tmp, self.trLen + truncLen, th); }
    let es = bytes.alloc(hl); tls.earlySecretPsk(self.kind, self.ticketPsk, hl, es);
    let bk = bytes.alloc(hl); tls.deriveResBinderKey(self.kind, es, bk);
    let fk = bytes.alloc(hl); handshake.finishedKey(self.kind, bk, fk);
    let binder = bytes.alloc(hl);
    if (self.kind == 1) { hmac.hmacSha384(fk, hl, th, hl, binder); }
    else { hmac.hmacSha256(fk, hl, th, hl, binder); }
    let k = 0;
    while (k < hl) { bytes.write_byte(out, chLen - hl + k, bytes.read_byte(binder, k)); k = k + 1; }
    bytes.free(tmp); bytes.free(th); bytes.free(es); bytes.free(bk); bytes.free(fk); bytes.free(binder);
}

/// Parses a NewSessionTicket message and stores the resumption ticket and PSK.
///
/// `buf` holds the message and `off` is where it starts. It reads (and skips)
/// the ticket lifetime, then captures `ticket_age_add`, the ticket nonce, and
/// the ticket bytes, copying the ticket into a freshly sized owned buffer. It
/// then derives the resumption PSK for this ticket from the resumption master
/// secret and the nonce, records the hash `kind` in force, and sets
/// `haveTicket`. After this the ticket and its PSK can seed a later
/// [`newClientResume`]. The extensions field of the ticket is not consumed here.
pub fn parseNewSessionTicket(self: TlsClient, buf: ptr, off: int): void {
    let p = off + 4;
    p = p + 4;
    let ageAdd = ((bytes.read_byte(buf, p) as long) << 24) | ((bytes.read_byte(buf, p + 1) as long) << 16)
               | ((bytes.read_byte(buf, p + 2) as long) << 8) | (bytes.read_byte(buf, p + 3) as long);
    p = p + 4;
    let nonceLen = bytes.read_byte(buf, p); p = p + 1;
    let noncePos = p; p = p + nonceLen;
    let tlen = (bytes.read_byte(buf, p) << 8) | bytes.read_byte(buf, p + 1); p = p + 2;
    let tpos = p;
    bytes.free(self.ticket);
    self.ticket = bytes.alloc(tlen);
    let i = 0;
    while (i < tlen) { bytes.write_byte(self.ticket, i, bytes.read_byte(buf, tpos + i)); i = i + 1; }
    self.ticketLen = tlen;
    self.ticketAgeAdd = ageAdd;
    self.ticketKind = self.kind;
    tls.deriveResumptionPsk(self.kind, self.resMaster, (buf as long + noncePos) as ptr, nonceLen, self.ticketPsk);
    self.haveTicket = true;
}

/// Pointer to the stored session ticket bytes, to present as a PSK identity on
/// resumption.
pub fn ticketPtr(self: TlsClient): ptr { return self.ticket; }
/// Length in bytes of the stored session ticket.
pub fn ticketSize(self: TlsClient): int { return self.ticketLen; }
/// Pointer to the resumption PSK derived for the stored ticket.
pub fn ticketPskPtr(self: TlsClient): ptr { return self.ticketPsk; }
/// The ticket's `ticket_age_add`, needed to compute the obfuscated ticket age
/// offered in a resumption ClientHello.
pub fn ticketAge(self: TlsClient): long { return self.ticketAgeAdd; }
/// The hash `kind` in force when the ticket was issued; a resumed handshake
/// must reuse it.
pub fn ticketHashKind(self: TlsClient): int { return self.ticketKind; }
/// Whether a usable session ticket has been stored (via
/// [`parseNewSessionTicket`] or [`newClientResume`]).
pub fn hasTicket(self: TlsClient): bool { return self.haveTicket; }
/// Whether the server accepted the offered PSK in this handshake (echoed the
/// pre_shared_key extension).
pub fn pskWasAccepted(self: TlsClient): bool { return self.pskAccepted; }


/// Derives the 0-RTT early-data traffic keys and enables the early-data offer.
///
/// Computes the early secret from the ticket PSK, hashes the transcript so far
/// (which at this point is just the ClientHello), derives the client
/// early-traffic secret and expands it into the early record key and IV. Resets
/// `eSeq` and sets `offerEarly`. Call this after building the resumption
/// ClientHello so early application data can be sent with [`encryptEarly`]
/// before the ServerHello arrives.
pub fn deriveEarlyKeys(self: TlsClient): void {
    let hl = trHashLen(self);
    let es = bytes.alloc(hl); tls.earlySecretPsk(self.kind, self.ticketPsk, hl, es);
    let chHash = bytes.alloc(hl); trHash(self, chHash);
    let cet = bytes.alloc(hl); tls.deriveEarlyTraffic(self.kind, es, chHash, cet);
    tls.deriveKeyIv(self.kind, self.suite, cet, self.eKey, self.eIv);
    self.eSeq = 0;
    self.offerEarly = true;
    bytes.free(es); bytes.free(chHash); bytes.free(cet);
}

/// Encrypts 0-RTT early application data into a protected record in `out`,
/// returning the record length.
///
/// Protects the plaintext as an application_data record (content type 0x17)
/// with the early key and IV derived by [`deriveEarlyKeys`], advancing `eSeq`.
/// Whether the server actually accepts this data is only known later, via
/// [`earlyWasAccepted`]; if it declines, the data must be resent under the
/// normal application keys.
pub fn encryptEarly(self: TlsClient, data: ptr, dataLen: int, out: ptr): int {
    let n = tls.recordProtect(self.suite, self.eKey, self.eIv, self.eSeq, data, dataLen, 0x17, out);
    self.eSeq = self.eSeq + 1;
    return n;
}

/// Builds and encrypts the EndOfEarlyData message into `out`, returning the
/// record length.
///
/// When the server has accepted early data, the client sends EndOfEarlyData to
/// mark the boundary before switching to handshake keys. The message is added
/// to the transcript via [`trAdd`] (it is part of the handshake) and protected
/// with the early key, advancing `eSeq`. Only sent on the 0-RTT-accepted path.
pub fn endOfEarlyDataRecord(self: TlsClient, out: ptr): int {
    let msg = bytes.alloc(4);
    bytes.write_byte(msg, 0, 0x05); bytes.write_byte(msg, 1, 0); bytes.write_byte(msg, 2, 0); bytes.write_byte(msg, 3, 0);
    trAdd(self, msg, 4);
    let n = tls.recordProtect(self.suite, self.eKey, self.eIv, self.eSeq, msg, 4, 0x16, out);
    self.eSeq = self.eSeq + 1;
    bytes.free(msg);
    return n;
}

/// Whether the server accepted the offered 0-RTT early data.
///
/// Set while processing EncryptedExtensions. If false, any data sent with
/// [`encryptEarly`] was discarded by the server and must be replayed under the
/// application keys.
pub fn earlyWasAccepted(self: TlsClient): bool { return self.earlyAccepted; }
